  NOTE 270 of 280 thermal heat rates were floored to physical minima (the dataset's gas heat rates are not physical)

==============================================================================
E4  Out-of-sample validation
==============================================================================
  Clear on one scenario set, then evaluate the resulting day-ahead
  commitment on a DISJOINT set drawn from the same conditional pool.
  This separates 'CVaR genuinely reduces tail loss' from 'CVaR fits the
  in-sample tail'. Differences are paired by replication.

  held-out loss, risk-aware MINUS risk-neutral (30 replications, train S=400, test S=400):
  negative = risk-aware is better out of sample
    E[l]            (expected)             52,567.34 +/-   5,342.37   improved in 0/30 replications
    CVaR_0.95[l]      (tail)             -219,601.62 +/-   2,743.52   improved in 30/30 replications
    rho_RA[l]       (planner obj)         -29,083.35 +/-   3,817.43   improved in 29/30 replications

  The tail column is the claim that matters: if CVaR clearing only fit
  the in-sample tail, this would not be reliably negative.

